    {
      "id": "o8p1LH90FA07OsazVtcYV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1543.1611598332618,
      "y": 63.13991624067967,
      "width": 745.1612577027756,
      "height": 2800,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#da77f2",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBZ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1041999971,
      "version": 3378,
      "versionNonce": 2128869171,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781027608947,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Video Processing\n- Streaming Technology Stack\n    > Adaptive Protocols: HLS (HTTP Live Streaming), DASH \n    > Codecs: H.264, HEVC (Efficient compression/decompression)\n    > Objective: Drastically reduce raw file sizes to minimize storage and\ndelivery costs.\n\n- Asynchronous Processing Architecture\n    > Message Queues (Amazon SQS, RabbitMQ) separate the fast upload\nprocess from the slow, long-running video processing tasks.\n\n- Processing Pipeline Stages\n    > File Chunker: Splits file to eliminate duplicates and reduce network\nload.\n    > Mechanism Trade-off: Timestamp-based (equal splits) vs. Scene-based\n(Netflix approach: splits by scene to prevent mid-scene buffering\ninterruptions).\n    > Content Filter: ML models execute copyright, piracy, and NSFW\nvalidation.\n    > [Content Filter] --> |Violations| [Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ)] -->\n[Manual Moderation]\n    > Transcoder: Decodes to uncompressed intermediate format, then\nencodes to optimized target format (via FFmpeg, AWS Elemental\nMediaConvert). Adjusts bitrates and downsamples.\n    > Quality Conversion: Generates adaptive resolution variants (4K, 1440p,\n1080p, 720p).\n    > Storage: Final assets uploaded for streaming retrieval.\n    > Complete Pipeline Architecture Flow\n[Message Queue] --> [File Chunker] --> [Content Filter] --> [Transcoder] >\n[Quality Conversion] --> [Distributed Storage (S3 / HDFS)]\n\n2) Streaming \n- Standard CDN Integration\n    > Purpose: Minimize latency by caching content at distributed edge\nservers globally.\n    > Setup: Secure storage-to-CDN links, dynamic caching (based on\nbandwidth/views), geo-replication, performance monitoring.\n\n- Netflix Open Connect (Proprietary CDN)\n    > Purpose: Bypasses traditional CDNs by placing custom hardware\ndirectly inside Internet Service Providers (ISPs).\n    > Scale: Open Connect Appliances (OCAs) in 1000+ locations handle\n~95% of global traffic.\n    > Innovations: Custom high-volume hardware, direct ISP peering,\nminimized routing distance.\n\n- Adaptive Bitrate Streaming\n    > Protocol: HTTP Live Streaming (HLS).\n    > Mechanism: Dynamically adapts video quality (bitrate/resolution) to\nmatch the user's real-time internet connection speed, ensuring uninterrupted\nplayback.\n\n3) Pausing, Future Playing \n- Resume Playback Mechanism\n    > How it works: Uses the timestamp_left_off property stored in the\nuser's history table.\n    > Action: The client directly requests the exact scene chunk associated\nwith that timestamp to resume seamlessly.\n\n- Video Seeking (Jump & Buffer Management)\n    > Scenario: User pauses at 1:00 and jumps directly to 30:00.\n    > Manifest Lookup: The client player consults the streaming manifest\nfile (HLS/DASH index) to map the 30:00 timestamp to the exact\ncorresponding video chunk.\n    > Buffer Flush: The player intentionally drops/clears its existing\nforward buffer (e.g., the pre-fetched chunks for 1:01 to 1:05) to free up\nmemory and prevent playback of stale data.\n    > Targeted Fetch: The client initiates a fresh HTTP request specifically\nfor the new chunk starting at 30:00. CDN starts delivering immediately. \n\n4) Searching\n- Requirement: Fast, near real-time search across massive volumes of video\nmetadata (titles, tags) where traditional DBMS performance falls short.\nSolution: Elasticsearch\n- Architecture: Distributed search and analytics engine built on Apache\nLucene. Elastic. Updated regularly via CDC. \n\n5)  Geo-Blocking and Content Availability\n- Core Concept: Restricting video access based on regional licensing and\ncopyright laws by identifying the user's geographic location.\n\n- 4-Step Enforcement Strategy\n    > Locationh Detection: Cross-references public IP against geolocation\ndatabases. Uses DNS routing (e.g., Route53) to direct users or serve error\npages if in a blocked region.\n    > UI Catalog Filtering: Rights/Entitlement Service pre-filters the\ncatalog based on the user's region (UX optimization, not actual security).\n    > DRM Enforcement (The Gatekeeper): Videos are heavily encrypted.\nUpon clicking \"Play,\" the DRM License Server strictly re-verifies location\nbefore issuing the required decryption key.\n> Edge Delivery: CDNs (e.g., CloudFront) use native geographic restrictions\nto drop unauthorized regional requests right at the network edge,\nprotecting backend servers.\n\n- VPN Mitigation Strategies\n    > IP Blocklisting: Actively blocking known VPN and data center IP\nranges.\n    > Billing Verification: Cross-checking the IP location against the user's\nregistered payment method country.\n\n6) Recommender System\n- Core Algorithms\n    > Collaborative Filtering: Predictions based on the behavior of similar\nusers.\n    > Content-Based Filtering: Suggestions based on similar content the\nuser liked previously.\n    > Custom ML Models: Hybrid engines (e.g., Netflix Recommendation\nEngine) to refine accuracy.\n- Input Data Points\n    > Explicit: Ratings, feedback, search queries.\n    > Implicit: Viewing history, watch durations, browsing/scrolling behavior.\n    > Contextual: Device used, timestamp, user profile (age, location).",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Video Processing\n- Streaming Technology Stack\n    > Adaptive Protocols: HLS (HTTP Live Streaming), DASH \n    > Codecs: H.264, HEVC (Efficient compression/decompression)\n    > Objective: Drastically reduce raw file sizes to minimize storage and delivery costs.\n\n- Asynchronous Processing Architecture\n    > Message Queues (Amazon SQS, RabbitMQ) separate the fast upload process from the slow, long-running video processing tasks.\n\n- Processing Pipeline Stages\n    > File Chunker: Splits file to eliminate duplicates and reduce network load.\n    > Mechanism Trade-off: Timestamp-based (equal splits) vs. Scene-based (Netflix approach: splits by scene to prevent mid-scene buffering interruptions).\n    > Content Filter: ML models execute copyright, piracy, and NSFW validation.\n    > [Content Filter] --> |Violations| [Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ)] --> [Manual Moderation]\n    > Transcoder: Decodes to uncompressed intermediate format, then encodes to optimized target format (via FFmpeg, AWS Elemental MediaConvert). Adjusts bitrates and downsamples.\n    > Quality Conversion: Generates adaptive resolution variants (4K, 1440p, 1080p, 720p).\n    > Storage: Final assets uploaded for streaming retrieval.\n    > Complete Pipeline Architecture Flow\n[Message Queue] --> [File Chunker] --> [Content Filter] --> [Transcoder] > [Quality Conversion] --> [Distributed Storage (S3 / HDFS)]\n\n2) Streaming \n- Standard CDN Integration\n    > Purpose: Minimize latency by caching content at distributed edge servers globally.\n    > Setup: Secure storage-to-CDN links, dynamic caching (based on bandwidth/views), geo-replication, performance monitoring.\n\n- Netflix Open Connect (Proprietary CDN)\n    > Purpose: Bypasses traditional CDNs by placing custom hardware directly inside Internet Service Providers (ISPs).\n    > Scale: Open Connect Appliances (OCAs) in 1000+ locations handle ~95% of global traffic.\n    > Innovations: Custom high-volume hardware, direct ISP peering, minimized routing distance.\n\n- Adaptive Bitrate Streaming\n    > Protocol: HTTP Live Streaming (HLS).\n    > Mechanism: Dynamically adapts video quality (bitrate/resolution) to match the user's real-time internet connection speed, ensuring uninterrupted playback.\n\n3) Pausing, Future Playing \n- Resume Playback Mechanism\n    > How it works: Uses the timestamp_left_off property stored in the user's history table.\n    > Action: The client directly requests the exact scene chunk associated with that timestamp to resume seamlessly.\n\n- Video Seeking (Jump & Buffer Management)\n    > Scenario: User pauses at 1:00 and jumps directly to 30:00.\n    > Manifest Lookup: The client player consults the streaming manifest file (HLS/DASH index) to map the 30:00 timestamp to the exact corresponding video chunk.\n    > Buffer Flush: The player intentionally drops/clears its existing forward buffer (e.g., the pre-fetched chunks for 1:01 to 1:05) to free up memory and prevent playback of stale data.\n    > Targeted Fetch: The client initiates a fresh HTTP request specifically for the new chunk starting at 30:00. CDN starts delivering immediately. \n\n4) Searching\n- Requirement: Fast, near real-time search across massive volumes of video metadata (titles, tags) where traditional DBMS performance falls short.\nSolution: Elasticsearch\n- Architecture: Distributed search and analytics engine built on Apache Lucene. Elastic. Updated regularly via CDC. \n\n5)  Geo-Blocking and Content Availability\n- Core Concept: Restricting video access based on regional licensing and copyright laws by identifying the user's geographic location.\n\n- 4-Step Enforcement Strategy\n    > Locationh Detection: Cross-references public IP against geolocation databases. Uses DNS routing (e.g., Route53) to direct users or serve error pages if in a blocked region.\n    > UI Catalog Filtering: Rights/Entitlement Service pre-filters the catalog based on the user's region (UX optimization, not actual security).\n    > DRM Enforcement (The Gatekeeper): Videos are heavily encrypted. Upon clicking \"Play,\" the DRM License Server strictly re-verifies location before issuing the required decryption key.\n> Edge Delivery: CDNs (e.g., CloudFront) use native geographic restrictions to drop unauthorized regional requests right at the network edge, protecting backend servers.\n\n- VPN Mitigation Strategies\n    > IP Blocklisting: Actively blocking known VPN and data center IP ranges.\n    > Billing Verification: Cross-checking the IP location against the user's registered payment method country.\n\n6) Recommender System\n- Core Algorithms\n    > Collaborative Filtering: Predictions based on the behavior of similar users.\n    > Content-Based Filtering: Suggestions based on similar content the user liked previously.\n    > Custom ML Models: Hybrid engines (e.g., Netflix Recommendation Engine) to refine accuracy.\n- Input Data Points\n    > Explicit: Ratings, feedback, search queries.\n    > Implicit: Viewing history, watch durations, browsing/scrolling behavior.\n    > Contextual: Device used, timestamp, user profile (age, location).",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
